---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Delivery Framework Cheat Sheet" track="sd">
  <h1>⚡ The 45-Minute Delivery Framework</h1>
  <p class="lede">Print this into your head. Every SD answer runs this exact spine — the phases and their time budgets never change, only the content does.</p>

  <div class="phase" data-min="0–5 min">
    <h3>1. Requirements</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Functional:</strong> "Users can…" — pick the 2–3 core flows, explicitly park the rest ("out of scope: analytics, admin").</li>
      <li><strong>Non-functional:</strong> the ones that change the design — scale (DAU, QPS), latency targets, consistency vs availability, durability.</li>
      <li>End by <em>saying the list back</em> and getting a nod.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="phase" data-min="5–10 min">
    <h3>2. Back-of-Envelope Estimation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Only compute what changes a decision: QPS (÷10⁵ rule), storage (carry units up the ladder), memory working set (1 node vs cluster?), bandwidth (usually: decide what to ignore).</li>
      <li>State the conclusion out loud: "~12K QPS reads, so we need a cache tier; 3.6 TB/yr, so single Postgres is fine for years."</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="phase" data-min="10–15 min">
    <h3>3. API + Data Model</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Core endpoints only — method, path, request/response shape. REST unless the problem screams otherwise.</li>
      <li>Core entities + relationships. Call out the M:N joins explicitly (your known trap — say the join table out loud).</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="phase" data-min="15–30 min">
    <h3>4. High-Level Design</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Draw: client → LB/gateway → services → data stores. Trace ONE core flow end-to-end through the boxes.</li>
      <li>Every box earns its place — say why it exists in one sentence.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="phase" data-min="30–42 min">
    <h3>5. Deep Dives</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>2–3 hard parts. Lead with the one the interviewer poked at.</li>
      <li>Format per dive: problem → 2 options → tradeoff → <strong>commit to one</strong>. No hand-waves: cache means key shape + TTL + invalidation, or it doesn't count.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="phase" data-min="42–45 min">
    <h3>6. Wrap-Up</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Bottlenecks you'd attack next, what you'd monitor, what breaks at 10×.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <blockquote>
    <p><strong>Your personal red flags</strong> (from real mock data): ① answering a different layer than asked (identity ≠ tenant ≠ role ≠ resource), ② "cache it in Redis" without key/TTL/invalidation, ③ going silent instead of reasoning out loud, ④ skipping the estimation → design bridge sentence.</p>
  </blockquote>

  <style>
    .lede { color: var(--text-dim); }
    .phase {
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      background: var(--bg-raised);
      padding: 0.4rem 1.2rem 0.7rem;
      margin: 0.8rem 0;
      position: relative;
    }
    .phase::after {
      content: attr(data-min);
      position: absolute;
      top: 0.85rem;
      right: 1.1rem;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--accent);
    }
    .phase h3 { margin: 0.6rem 0 0.3rem; }
  </style>
</Base>
